Tolerate EINVAL, not just ESRCH, when pthread_kill fails signaling a
GC-registered thread during stop-the-world suspend or restart.

GC_suspend_all (pthread_stop_world.c) signals every registered-but-not-yet-
suspended thread to pause it for a collection cycle; GC_start_world signals
every suspended thread to resume it afterward. If the target thread exits
between GC discovering it in GC_threads and this pthread_kill call, the
result is undefined by POSIX: depending on glibc/kernel version, the kernel
may return the "textbook" ESRCH (which both switches already tolerate,
decrementing n_live_threads and moving on) -- or it may instead return
EINVAL, which both switches treat as a hard, unconditional abort() via
ABORT_ARG1.

Confirmed via nanopynix's own multithreaded EvalState test suite under
ThreadSanitizer (which slows execution enough to make this race land
reliably): every observed abort here reports errcode=22 (EINVAL), never
ESRCH, immediately following legitimate GC allocation/collection activity
with no other explanation. First observed at suspend time
("pthread_kill failed at suspend"); after patching that site alone, the
same race reappeared at the symmetric restart site
("pthread_kill failed at resume"), confirming this is one race reachable
from both of GC's stop-the-world signal paths, not something specific to
suspension.

GC_sig_suspend and GC_sig_thr_restart are both fixed, valid signal numbers
for the entire lifetime of the process (computed once at GC_thr_init), so
EINVAL from pthread_kill on either cannot mean "invalid signal number" in
this codebase -- the only realistic cause is the same already-tolerated
already-gone-thread race, just surfacing under a different errno on this
glibc/kernel combination. This exact race (pthread_kill vs. thread
teardown, undefined by POSIX) is a known, previously-discussed bdwgc/glibc
issue upstream; treating EINVAL the same as ESRCH here is the same
tolerance both switches already apply, just widened to the errno this
environment actually produces.

The tolerated branch also logs, under `GC_PRINT_STATS=1`. Tolerating the
race removes an abort that killed the process, and it leaves behind a
thread that this collection did not suspend and whose stack it did not
push. When the thread really is gone that is correct. When it is not, the
collection frees whatever only that stack refers to, and the next read of
one of those objects gives a value of the wrong type. That is the shape of
issue #70, and the branch reported nothing at all until now: bdwgc's own
`Resent %d signals after timeout` and `Lost some threads while stopping or
starting world?!` cover the retry path and not this one.

`GC_COND_LOG_PRINTF` writes only when `GC_print_stats` is set, which is what
`GC_PRINT_STATS=1` sets, so a normal run prints nothing. The same file
already logs from this machinery at the retry site.

